Feature: Signal Route Generation
  Signal routes are generated by traversing the railway network from each stop signal
  to the next stop signal in each valid direction. Each signal route connects two
  consecutive stop signals and references the segment profiles that make up the route.

  Background:
    Given a railway network loaded from FacilityData
    And the network has nodes, links, and signals
    And node paths connect all link pairs at each node

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Signal Route traversal
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Scenario: Generate signal routes from stop signals
    Given the network contains stop signals of types INF, UBL, M, and MBL
    When signal routes are generated
    Then for each stop signal on each link
    And for each valid direction (Med, Mot) from that signal
    Then the network is traversed from that signal until the next stop signal is found
    And a signal route is created from the start signal to the end signal

  Scenario: Signal route contains segment profile references
    Given a signal route from signal A to signal B
    When the route traverses one or more links
    Then the signal route contains a SegmentProfileRef for each link usage
    And each SegmentProfileRef has a SPID composed of the link ID and direction
    And each SegmentProfileRef has a StartPos and EndPos within the link
    And each SegmentProfileRef has the traversal Direction

  Scenario: Signal route records start and end places
    Given a signal route from signal A to signal B
    Then the signal route StartPlace is the location signature of signal A
    And the signal route EndPlace is the location signature of signal B

  Scenario: Visited links prevent infinite traversal
    Given the network may contain cycles through node paths
    When traversing the network for a signal route
    Then each link is visited at most once per traversal
    And visiting an already-visited link stops that branch of the traversal

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Signal type classification
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Scenario Outline: Signal types are classified as line or station signals
    Given a signal of type "<SignalType>"
    Then the signal is classified as a "<Classification>" signal

    Examples:
      | SignalType | Classification |
      | INF        | Line           |
      | UBL        | Line           |
      | MBL        | Line           |
      | M          | Station        |


Feature: Track Route Generation
  Track routes group signal routes by type, place, and track number to enable
  lookup from timetable data (place + track) to segment profiles. Each track route
  is classified as Arrival, Departure, or Line based on the signal types at its
  start and end.

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Track route classification
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Scenario Outline: Classify signal route into track route type
    Given a signal route starting at a "<StartSignalClass>" signal
    And ending at a "<EndSignalClass>" signal
    Then the track route type is "<TrackRouteType>"

    Examples:
      | StartSignalClass | EndSignalClass | TrackRouteType |
      | Line             | Station        | Arrival        |
      | Station          | Line           | Departure      |
      | Line             | Line           | Line           |

  Scenario: Station-to-station route at same place defaults to Arrival
    Given a signal route from station signal M at place "X"
    And ending at station signal M at the same place "X"
    Then the track route type is "Arrival"

  Scenario: Station-to-station route at different places becomes Line
    Given a signal route from station signal M at place "X"
    And ending at station signal M at a different place "Y"
    Then the track route type is "Line"

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Track number resolution
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Scenario: Line signal uses linjespår for track number
    Given a line signal (INF, UBL, or MBL) at a position on a link
    When resolving the track number
    Then the linjespår covering that position on the link is used
    And spårnummer is never used as fallback

  Scenario: Station signal uses spårnummer for track number
    Given a station signal (M) at a position on a link
    When resolving the track number
    Then the spårnummer covering that position on the link is used
    And linjespår is never used as fallback

  Scenario: Search forward for start track when not found at signal position
    Given a signal route where the start signal's link has no matching track data
    When resolving the start track
    Then links are searched forward along the route
    And the first matching track of the correct type (linjespår or spårnummer) is used

  Scenario: Search backward for end track when not found at signal position
    Given a signal route where the end signal's link has no matching track data
    When resolving the end track
    Then links are searched backward along the route
    And the first matching track of the correct type (linjespår or spårnummer) is used

  Scenario: Every track route has both StartTrack and EndTrack
    When track routes are generated
    Then every track route has a non-empty StartTrack
    And every track route has a non-empty EndTrack

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Track route grouping
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Scenario: Signal routes are grouped into track routes
    Given classified signal routes with resolved track numbers
    When track routes are generated
    Then signal routes with the same Type, StartPlace, EndPlace, StartTrack, and EndTrack are grouped together
    And each group becomes one track route

  Scenario: Track route references its signal routes
    Given a track route grouped from one or more signal routes
    Then the track route contains a SignalRouteRef for each signal route in the group
    And each SignalRouteRef has the StartSignal and EndSignal of the signal route

  Scenario: Track route has start and end signals
    Given a track route grouped from signal routes
    Then the track route StartSignal is the StartSignal of the first signal route
    And the track route EndSignal is the EndSignal of the last signal route

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Track route attributes by type
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  Scenario: Arrival track route attributes
    Given an Arrival track route
    Then StartPlace and EndPlace are the same station
    And StartTrack is a linjespår (e.g. "E", "E1")
    And EndTrack is a spårnummer (e.g. "1", "2")

  Scenario: Departure track route attributes
    Given a Departure track route
    Then StartPlace and EndPlace are the same station
    And StartTrack is a spårnummer (e.g. "1", "2")
    And EndTrack is a linjespår (e.g. "E", "E1")

  Scenario: Line track route attributes
    Given a Line track route
    Then StartPlace and EndPlace are different stations
    And StartTrack is a linjespår (e.g. "E", "E1")
    And EndTrack is a linjespår (e.g. "E", "E1")
